E.append(terr("territory-basilar", "Basilar (pontine) territory", "territory-basilar-paramedian", ["artery-basilar", "arteries-pontine", "artery-aica", "artery-sca"],
 "The basilar territory is the pons and the ventral midbrain supplied by the paramedian, short circumferential and long circumferential branches of the basilar artery. This level-1 domain covers the whole pons, so it includes the paramedian pontine lacunes of small vessel disease (pure motor hemiparesis, ataxic hemiparesis, dysarthria-clumsy hand), the lateral pontine syndromes of the circumferential branches (Millard-Gubler, Foville and the AICA syndrome), and the bilateral ventral pontine infarction of basilar occlusion that produces the locked-in state. The paramedian branches are end-arteries with no collateral, which is why the pons is the commonest brainstem site of lacunes and of hypertensive hemorrhage.",
 "The entire pons (basis and tegmentum) and the ventral and paramedian midbrain, from the pontomedullary junction to the interpeduncular fossa, including the middle cerebellar peduncle's proximal part; paramedian branches supply the medial basis and tegmentum, short circumferential branches the lateral basis, and long circumferential branches (AICA, SCA) the lateral tegmentum and peduncles; bounded below by the vertebral and PICA territories of the medulla and above by the PCA thalamic and midbrain domains.",
 "The territory contains the corticospinal and corticopontine fibers of the basis pontis, the pontine nuclei, the medial lemniscus and MLF, the abducens and facial nuclei and the pontine gaze center, the trigeminal nuclei, the vestibular and cochlear nuclei, the locus coeruleus and the pontine reticular formation that sustains consciousness and breathing, so ischemia produces the crossed pontine syndromes (ipsilateral VI or VII palsy with contralateral hemiparesis), internuclear ophthalmoplegia and gaze palsy, and, when bilateral, coma or the locked-in state. Thrombosis of the basilar trunk, embolism to its top and hypertensive lipohyalinosis of its perforators are the three mechanisms.",
 [mview("axial", "territory-basilar-l", "basilar territory of the pons"), mview("sagittal", "territory-basilar-r", "pontine domain of the basilar perforators")],
 "On the overlay the basilar domain covers the pons and ventral midbrain; on imaging a paramedian pontine infarct is a wedge of restricted diffusion reaching the ventral surface and stopping at the midline.",
 [pathol("Paramedian pontine infarct", "MRI", "Unilateral wedge of restricted diffusion in the basis pontis reaching the ventral surface with pure motor or ataxic hemiparesis; early DWI may be negative.", sequence="DWI"),
  pathol("Basilar occlusion with bilateral pontine infarction", "MRI", "Bilateral ventral pontine restricted diffusion with locked-in syndrome or coma; hyperdense basilar on CT and absent flow on CTA.", sequence="DWI"),
  pathol("Pontine hemorrhage", "CT", "Central pontine hyperdensity with coma, pinpoint reactive pupils, quadriplegia and ocular bobbing; large hemorrhages are fatal.", sequence="CT non-contrast")],
 [("Contralateral hemiparesis with ipsilateral VI or VII palsy", "ipsilateral", "Paramedian and lateral pontine infarction (crossed syndromes)"), ("Internuclear ophthalmoplegia and horizontal gaze palsy", "ipsilateral", "MLF and pontine gaze center infarction"), ("Locked-in syndrome", "bilateral", "Bilateral basis pontis infarction sparing the tegmentum"), ("Coma with pinpoint pupils", "bilateral", "Pontine tegmental destruction")],
 ["Horizontal eye movements, facial strength and corneal reflexes", "Strength and coordination on the side opposite the cranial nerve signs", "Consciousness, pupils and breathing in bilateral disease"],
 ["Pontine lacunes mimic capsular lacunes; the eyes and the face give them away", "Locked-in patients are awake: ask them to blink or look up before you decide they are comatose", "The basilar perforators have no collaterals and no forgiveness"],
 VC + [R("sp-basilar-artery"), R("sp-basilar-artery-occlusion"), R("sp-vertebrobasilar-system")], synonyms=["pontine territory", "basilar perforator domain"], syndromes=["syn-basilar-occlusion", "syn-locked-in", "syn-millard-gubler", "syn-foville", "syn-pontine-hemorrhage", "syn-lacunar-ataxic-hemiparesis", "syn-lacunar-dysarthria-clumsy-hand"],
 afferents=[("Paramedian, short circumferential and long circumferential branches of the basilar artery", "arterial supply")], efferents=[("Anterior pontomesencephalic vein and petrosal veins", "venous drainage")]))

E.append(terr("territory-superior-cerebellar", "Superior cerebellar artery territory", "territory-sca", ["artery-sca", "artery-basilar"],
 "The superior cerebellar artery territory is the superior surface of the cerebellum, the anterior lobe and upper posterior lobe with the superior vermis, together with the dentate nucleus, the superior cerebellar peduncle and the dorsolateral upper pons and lower midbrain. It is the territory of the SCA syndrome: ipsilateral limb ataxia and dysarthria, contralateral loss of pain and temperature from the lateral tegmentum, and, in large infarcts, cerebellar swelling with upward transtentorial herniation. It borders the AICA and PICA territories on the cerebellar surface in a variable watershed, and its cortical branches receive collaterals from those arteries, so its infarcts are often smaller than the full domain.",
 "Superior surface of both cerebellar hemispheres and the superior vermis (lobules I-VI, upper Crus I), the deep white matter with the dentate nucleus, the superior cerebellar peduncle and the dorsolateral tegmentum of the rostral pons and caudal midbrain; bounded below by the AICA and PICA territories at the horizontal fissure region and medially by the basilar territory of the pons.",
 "The territory contains the anterior lobe's sensorimotor cortex for the limbs and trunk, the cerebellar outflow through the dentate nucleus and superior peduncle, the spinothalamic tract and the lateral lemniscus in the upper pontine tegmentum, the superior cerebellar peduncle's decussation and the trochlear nucleus region, so its infarction produces ipsilateral ataxia and intention tremor, dysarthria, contralateral spinothalamic sensory loss, and sometimes a contralateral trochlear palsy or Horner syndrome; its infarcts are usually embolic and often accompany PCA infarcts from the basilar tip. Swelling of a large SCA infarct compresses the midbrain and the aqueduct from above.",
 [mview("coronal", "territory-superior-cerebellar-l", "superior cerebellar artery territory on the upper cerebellum"), mview("sagittal", "territory-superior-cerebellar-r", "SCA domain of the superior vermis and anterior lobe")],
 "On the overlay the SCA domain covers the superior cerebellar surface beneath the tentorium and the upper pontine tegmentum; on imaging an SCA infarct is a wedge of the superior cerebellum reaching the tentorial surface.",
 [pathol("SCA infarct", "MRI", "Restricted diffusion of the superior cerebellar hemisphere and vermis with ipsilateral ataxia, dysarthria and contralateral pain-temperature loss; often with PCA infarcts.", sequence="DWI"),
  pathol("Upward transtentorial herniation", "CT", "Superior cerebellar swelling effacing the quadrigeminal cistern and flattening the tectum, with hydrocephalus and coma.", sequence="CT non-contrast", timing="Days 2-4 after a large infarct"),
  pathol("Cerebellar hemorrhage of the superior hemisphere", "CT", "Hyperdense superior cerebellar hematoma with acute ataxia, vomiting and headache.", sequence="CT non-contrast")],
 [("Ipsilateral limb ataxia and intention tremor", "ipsilateral", "Anterior lobe, dentate and superior peduncle infarction"), ("Dysarthria", "ipsilateral", "Paravermian and dentate outflow infarction"), ("Contralateral loss of pain and temperature", "contralateral", "Spinothalamic tract in the lateral upper pontine tegmentum"), ("Coma from upward herniation", "bilateral", "Swelling of the superior cerebellum against the midbrain")],
 ["Finger-nose, heel-shin and speech", "Pin-prick on the side opposite the ataxia", "Serial level of consciousness for three days after a large infarct"],
 ["The SCA is the cerebellar artery of the arms and speech; PICA is the artery of vertigo and falls", "Superior cerebellar swelling herniates upward: the tectum, not the tonsils, is flattened", "An SCA infarct without a PCA infarct is unusual; look for the basilar tip embolus"],
 VC + [R("sp-cerebellar-infarction"), R("sp-vertebrobasilar-system"), R("sp-dentate-nucleus")], synonyms=["SCA territory", "superior cerebellar domain"], syndromes=["syn-sca-infarct", "syn-cerebellar-hemispheric", "syn-top-of-basilar"],
 afferents=[("Superior cerebellar artery (medial and lateral branches)", "arterial supply")], efferents=[("Superior cerebellar veins to the great cerebral vein and the straight sinus", "venous drainage")]))

E.append(terr("territory-inferior-cerebellar", "Inferior cerebellar (PICA/AICA) territory", "territory-pica-lateral-medulla", ["artery-pica", "artery-aica", "artery-vertebral", "artery-basilar"],
 "The inferior cerebellar territory combines the domains of the posterior inferior and anterior inferior cerebellar arteries: the inferior and posterior cerebellar surface, the tonsils, the inferior vermis with the nodulus, the flocculus, and the lateral medulla and lateral caudal pons. It is the territory of the lateral medullary (Wallenberg) syndrome and the lateral pontine (AICA) syndrome, of the isolated vertigo of medial PICA infarction that mimics vestibular neuritis, and of the large PICA infarct that swells against the fourth ventricle and herniates the tonsils. Its arteries arise from the vertebral (PICA) and basilar (AICA) trunks and are occluded by vertebral dissection, atherosclerosis and embolism.",
 "Posterior and inferior cerebellar surface (Crus II, lobules VII-X, the tonsils and inferior vermis) from PICA, the anterior inferior surface with the flocculus and the middle cerebellar peduncle region from AICA, the lateral medulla (PICA and vertebral perforators) and the lateral caudal pontine tegmentum and inner ear (AICA and labyrinthine artery); bounded above by the SCA territory and medially by the medullary and pontine paramedian territories.",
 "The territory holds the vestibulocerebellum and the vestibular nuclei, the lateral medullary tegmentum with the spinal trigeminal tract, the spinothalamic tract, the nucleus ambiguus and the descending sympathetic fibers, the lateral pontine tegmentum with the facial and cochlear nuclei, and the inner ear, so ischemia produces vertigo, nystagmus and truncal ataxia, the crossed sensory loss and bulbar palsy of Wallenberg syndrome, or the facial palsy and deafness of the AICA syndrome; the PICA domain of the inferior cerebellum swells dangerously because the posterior fossa has no room. Collaterals between PICA, AICA and SCA over the surface vary and determine the infarct's size.",
 [mview("axial", "territory-inferior-cerebellar-l", "inferior cerebellar territory of PICA and AICA"), mview("sagittal", "territory-inferior-cerebellar-r", "posteroinferior cerebellar domain and lateral medulla")],
 "On the overlay the inferior cerebellar domain covers the posteroinferior cerebellum, the tonsils, the flocculus and the lateral medulla; on imaging a PICA infarct is a wedge of the posteroinferior hemisphere and an AICA infarct a small anterolateral lesion near the middle cerebellar peduncle.",
 [pathol("PICA infarct", "MRI", "Restricted diffusion of the posteroinferior cerebellum, sometimes with the lateral medulla; vertigo, ataxia and risk of swelling at 2-4 days.", sequence="DWI", timing="Swelling peaks on day 3; watch the fourth ventricle and tonsils"),
  pathol("AICA infarct", "MRI", "Restricted diffusion of the anterior inferior cerebellum and middle cerebellar peduncle with vertigo, ipsilateral deafness and facial palsy.", sequence="DWI"),
  pathol("Vertebral artery dissection", "MRA", "Tapered V2-V3 stenosis with intramural hematoma in a young patient with neck pain; PICA territory infarcts.", sequence="TOF")],
 [("Vertigo, nystagmus and truncal ataxia", "ipsilateral", "Vestibulocerebellar and vestibular nuclear ischemia"), ("Wallenberg syndrome: crossed sensory loss, dysphagia, Horner syndrome", "ipsilateral", "Lateral medullary infarction"), ("Ipsilateral deafness and facial palsy (AICA)", "ipsilateral", "Labyrinthine artery and lateral pontine infarction"), ("Tonsillar herniation and hydrocephalus", "bilateral", "Swelling of a large inferior cerebellar infarct")],
 ["HINTS examination and ability to stand", "Facial sensation, palate, Horner syndrome and hearing", "Serial consciousness and gaze for 72 hours"],
 ["Vertigo with a normal head impulse is PICA territory until proved otherwise", "Deafness with vertigo is AICA; vertigo alone is PICA", "The posterior fossa has no spare room: a swollen PICA infarct needs a surgeon on day two, not day four"],
 VC + [R("sp-anterior-inferior-cerebellar-arteries"), R("sp-cerebellar-infarction"), R("sp-vertebrobasilar-system")], synonyms=["PICA/AICA territory", "inferior cerebellar domain"], syndromes=["syn-pica-cerebellar-infarct", "syn-wallenberg-lateral-medullary", "syn-aica-lateral-pontine", "syn-central-vs-peripheral-vertigo", "syn-tonsillar-herniation"],
 afferents=[("Posterior inferior cerebellar artery from the vertebral artery; anterior inferior cerebellar artery from the basilar artery", "arterial supply")], efferents=[("Inferior cerebellar veins to the transverse and sigmoid sinuses", "venous drainage")]))

E.append(cortex("arteries-mra-atlas", "Cerebral arteries (MRA atlas iso-surface)", "x",
 "This mesh is the intracranial arterial tree extracted from a population magnetic resonance angiography atlas: the iso-surface of the average vessel probability map, thresholded within the brain, so it shows the vessels that are constant across people, the internal carotid siphons, the circle of Willis, the proximal anterior, middle and posterior cerebral arteries with their first cortical branches, the basilar and vertebral arteries and the cerebellar arteries. It is the atlas's imaging-derived counterpart to the named artery meshes registered from the whole-body model: it sits exactly on the MNI template so that it overlays the MRI slices without registration error, and it shows where the arteries really run relative to the brain surface and the cisterns.",
 "Intracranial arteries from the petrous carotid and the vertebrobasilar junction up to the second- and third-order cortical branches, following the basal cisterns, the Sylvian fissures, the interhemispheric fissure and the ambient cisterns, in MNI space with the brain mask as the limit.",
 "The tree gives the spatial relation of each artery to the parenchyma it supplies and to the cranial nerves and cisterns it crosses: the M1 segment along the anterior perforated substance where the lenticulostriates leave it, the A1 and anterior communicating complex above the chiasm, the P2 segment around the peduncle beside the third nerve, the basilar trunk in front of the pons, and the vertebral arteries at the medulla. Because it is an average, it is thicker and smoother than any individual's vessels and it omits variants (fetal PCA, hypoplastic A1), but it is the correct reference for locating aneurysm sites, thrombectomy targets and perforator origins on the atlas MRI.",
 ["artery-internal-carotid", "artery-basilar", "artery-vertebral", "artery-mca", "artery-aca", "artery-pca"],
 [mview("axial", "arteries-mra-atlas", "circle of Willis in the suprasellar cistern"), mview("coronal", "arteries-mra-atlas", "carotid siphons and the basilar artery in front of the brainstem")],
 "On time-of-flight MRA the intracranial arteries are bright flow signal; the atlas iso-surface reproduces the average of many such angiograms, with vessel caliber proportional to how consistently the vessel is present.",
 [pathol("Aneurysm at a circle of Willis branch point", "CTA", "Saccular outpouching at the anterior communicating, posterior communicating or MCA bifurcation, the three commonest sites, each visible on the atlas tree.", sequence="n/a"),
  pathol("Large vessel occlusion", "CTA", "Abrupt cut-off of the M1, basilar or carotid terminus with absent distal opacification; the atlas tree shows the expected course of the missing segment.", sequence="n/a"),
  pathol("Moyamoya disease", "MRA", "Progressive stenosis of the terminal carotid and proximal ACA and MCA with a puff-of-smoke network of collaterals.", sequence="TOF")],
 [("No deficit: reference mesh", "n/a", "This is an imaging-derived reference, not a structure with a lesion syndrome"), ("Localizes aneurysm and occlusion sites", "n/a", "The tree marks branch points and segments used in vascular localization")],
 ["Compare a patient's angiogram with the atlas tree to name segments and branch points", "Use the tree with the territory overlays to relate an occlusion to its expected infarct"],
 ["The MRA tree lies exactly on the atlas MRI; the registered whole-body arteries carry a few millimeters of error", "Aneurysms grow at branch points: the atlas tree shows the three commonest", "An average artery is thicker than a real one; use the tree for course, not caliber"],
 [R("sp-brain-arteries"), R("sp-circle-of-willis"), R("sp-cerebral-blood-supply")], synonyms=["intracranial arterial tree", "MRA average vessel map"], system="arteries", subsystem="mra", parent="circle-of-willis", mesh_ids=["arteries-mra-atlas"], territories=[], pathways=[], syndromes=["syn-subarachnoid-hemorrhage", "syn-basilar-occlusion", "syn-mca-stem-infarct"],
 afferents=[("Internal carotid and vertebral arteries", "inflow")], efferents=[("Cortical, perforating and cerebellar branches", "outflow")], tags=["reference", "MRA", "atlas"]))

E.append(cortex("brain-envelope", "Brain surface (template mask)", "x",
 "This mesh is the outer surface of the MNI152 template brain, generated from the template's brain mask, and it is the translucent shell that the atlas shows by default around every other structure. It represents the pial surface of the population-average brain including the cerebellum and brainstem, smoothed by averaging, so its sulci are shallow; it coincides exactly with the MRI slices and it is the reference against which the registered whole-body meshes are checked. In syndrome mode it stays visible while the parcels are dimmed, giving the lesion marker and the involved structures a frame, and it defines the field of view of the atlas.",
 "The boundary of the template brain mask: cerebral hemispheres, cerebellum and brainstem to the foramen magnum, in MNI space at 1 mm resolution, closed and smoothed.",
 "The envelope shows the position of any structure relative to the brain surface and the skull base, marks the tentorial and interhemispheric contours, and provides the visual container for the syndrome, pathway and quiz displays; its coincidence with the MRI means that a slice through it always shows brain at the surface, which makes it the natural reference for judging whether a registered mesh sits correctly. It also gives the scale of the atlas, roughly 140 mm wide, 170 mm long and 120 mm high for the average adult brain, and it is the surface used for camera framing.",
 ["artery-mca", "artery-aca", "artery-pca"],
 [mview("sagittal", "brain-envelope", "template brain surface on the midline"), mview("axial", "brain-envelope", "brain envelope around the hemispheres")],
 "The template brain mask boundary follows the pial surface of the average brain on all slices; individual gyri are visible only as shallow undulations because averaging smooths them.",
 [pathol("No pathology", "MRI", "The envelope is a reference surface without disease; a patient's brain surface departs from it with atrophy (widened sulci, retracted surface) or mass effect (displaced surface).", sequence="T1"),
  pathol("Registration quality control", "MRI", "Registered whole-body meshes that protrude beyond the envelope reveal the residual error of the affine transform.", sequence="T1")],
 [("No deficit: reference surface", "n/a", "Context envelope without a lesion syndrome")],
 ["Use the envelope as the visual frame for deep structures and syndromes", "Check registered meshes against it for alignment"],
 ["The envelope is the average brain's skin; everything in the atlas lives inside it", "When a registered nerve pokes through the envelope, blame the registration, not the anatomy", "The envelope and the MRI are the same data seen two ways"],
 [R("sp-brain"), R("sp-cerebral-cortex")], synonyms=["template brain surface", "pial envelope"], system="envelope", subsystem="template", parent=False, mesh_ids=["brain-envelope"], territories=[], tags=["reference", "envelope", "template"]))

write(E)
